# Logging and Error Diagnostics Standards

## Context
- When implementing logging in any part of the codebase
- When handling errors in critical operations
- When debugging complex operations
- When adding new on-chain or off-chain components

## Requirements

### On-Chain Program Logging
- Use ONLY `solana_program::msg!` for on-chain programs
- Keep messages concise to minimize compute units
- Log critical state changes and validations
- Include relevant account keys and data sizes
- Format for on-chain logs:
  ```rust
  solana_program::msg!("ComponentName: Action - Details");
  ```

### Off-Chain Code Logging
- Use `tracing` crate for all off-chain code
- Follow hierarchical logging levels:
  - `error!` - Unrecoverable errors
  - `warn!` - Recoverable issues needing attention
  - `info!` - Important state changes
  - `debug!` - Detailed development information
  - `trace!` - Very detailed debugging
- Include structured fields for machine parsing
- Use spans for operation context

### Error Diagnostics
- Include contextual data in error messages
- Log both error details and relevant state
- Provide component-specific error information
- Track error codes and their meanings
- Include system state in error logs
- Log relevant metrics with errors
- Implement hierarchical error logging
- Include operation metadata in error context
- Preserve error chain information

## Examples

### On-Chain Good Examples
<example>
```rust
// Good: Concise, informative on-chain logging
solana_program::msg!("TokenSwap: Initializing pool - tokens={}", pool_token_count);
solana_program::msg!("TokenSwap: Validating accounts - count={}", accounts.len());

// Error case with relevant context
if !account.is_signer {
    solana_program::msg!("TokenSwap: Invalid signer - expected={}, actual={}", 
        expected_key, account.key);
    return Err(ProgramError::MissingRequiredSignature);
}
```
</example>

### Off-Chain Good Examples
<example>
```rust
// Good: Structured off-chain logging with context
use tracing::{error, info, warn, debug, trace, instrument};

#[instrument(skip(config))]
async fn process_transaction(tx: Transaction, config: &Config) {
    info!(
        tx_id = %tx.signature,
        "Processing transaction"
    );
    
    if let Err(e) = validate_transaction(&tx) {
        error!(
            error = %e,
            tx_id = %tx.signature,
            "Transaction validation failed"
        );
    }
    
    debug!(
        tx_size = tx.data.len(),
        accounts = ?tx.accounts,
        "Transaction details"
    );
}

// Good: Comprehensive error logging
error!(
    "Proof verification failed - Details:\n\
     - Error: {:?}\n\
     - Input count: {}\n\
     - Total proof size: {} bytes\n\
     - Component hashes:\n\
       * proof_a: {}\n\
       * proof_b: {}\n\
     - First input preview: {}",
    e,
    input_count,
    proof_size,
    hex::encode(hash_a),
    hex::encode(hash_b),
    hex::encode(first_input)
);
```
</example>

### Invalid Examples
<example type="invalid">
```rust
// Bad: Using tracing in on-chain code
#[program]
pub mod my_program {
    use super::*;
    
    pub fn initialize(ctx: Context<Initialize>) -> Result<()> {
        info!("Initializing program");  // WRONG: Using tracing in on-chain code
        Ok(())
    }
}

// Bad: Using println! or eprintln!
pub fn process_instruction() {
    println!("Processing...");  // WRONG: Using println in any production code
}

// Bad: Using solana_program::msg! in off-chain code
pub async fn process_block(block: Block) {
    solana_program::msg!("Processing block");  // WRONG: Using msg! off-chain
}

// Bad: Insufficient error context
error!("Verification failed: {:?}", e);  // Lacks context and state information
```
</example>

## Critical Notes
<critical>
- NEVER use tracing or log crates in on-chain programs
- NEVER use println!/eprintln! in production code
- NEVER use solana_program::msg! in off-chain code
- ALWAYS use appropriate log levels
- ALWAYS include relevant context in log messages
- ALWAYS consider compute units when logging on-chain
- ALWAYS include full error context in error logs
- ALWAYS preserve error chains when wrapping errors
</critical>

## Migration Notes
1. For existing on-chain programs:
   - Replace any tracing macros with solana_program::msg!
   - Remove any log/tracing dependencies from Cargo.toml
   - Verify compute unit consumption after adding logs

2. For existing off-chain code:
   - Replace println!/eprintln! with appropriate tracing macros
   - Add structured fields to log messages
   - Implement proper span hierarchy
   - Enhance error logging with full context 